.TH PHOTOC 1 "September 28, 2026" "photoc" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
photoc \- Unix-style photography tools for the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B photoc
.RI [ global-options ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B photoc
.I command
.B \-\-help
.br
.B photoc \-\-help
.br
.B photoc \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B photoc
is a command-line toolkit for photographers working with local files and
repeatable shell workflows.
It inspects metadata, summarizes collections, finds exact duplicates,
renames and organizes photos, compresses copies, and removes EXIF GPS tags.
Normal output goes to standard output; diagnostics go to standard error.
.PP
macOS and Linux are the initial supported platforms.
Image and metadata operations currently recognize JPEG files with
.B .jpg
or
.B .jpeg
extensions, ignoring case.
The
.B duplicates
command compares regular files of any type.
The
.B focus
command compares JPEG sharpness scores as a review aid.
.PP
Directory operations scan one directory level by default.
.B \-\-recursive
includes nested directories where supported.
Symlinks are not followed during scans.
Quote paths containing spaces.
Use
.B \-\-
to end option parsing before a path beginning with a dash.
Option values must be separate arguments; combined short flags and
.B \-\-option=value
syntax are not supported.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS compress
.B photoc compress
.I file|directory
.RI [ "\-\-quality 1-100" ]
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ "\-\-output-dir directory" ]
.br
.B photoc compress
.I file|directory
.BI "\-\-target " size
.RI [ "\-\-min-quality 1-100" ]
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ "\-\-output-dir directory" ]
.PP
Re-encode JPEGs into new copies using lossy compression.
Quality defaults to 80.
.B \-\-target
searches for the highest quality fitting the requested size, with a default
minimum quality of 20.
.B \-\-quality
and
.B \-\-target
cannot be combined;
.B \-\-min-quality
requires
.BR \-\-target .
Sizes are positive integers in bytes, optionally suffixed by
.BR B ,
.BR KB ,
.BR MB ,
.BR GB ,
.BR KiB ,
.BR MiB ,
or
.BR GiB .
Suffixes are case-sensitive.
Decimal units use powers of 1000; binary units use powers of 1024.
.PP
Output names insert
.B .compressed
before the original extension, preserving extension case.
Without
.BR \-\-output-dir ,
copies are placed beside sources.
An output directory preserves paths relative to the input directory.
.B \-\-recursive
requires directory input.
Existing outputs fail single-file operations and are skipped in directory mode.
Directory scans skip existing
.B .compressed
copies.
.PP
Report original/output sizes, chosen quality, and savings.
Re-encoding can increase file size.
An unreachable target writes a best-effort copy at the minimum quality and
returns status 1.
EXIF, including GPS, is retained where possible.
ICC profiles, XMP, and other original JPEG marker segments are not copied.
.SS exif
.B photoc exif
.I file
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.PP
Inspect one JPEG without modifying it.
Report filename/path, file size, dimensions, camera make/model, ISO, aperture,
exposure time, focal length, capture timestamp, and available GPS coordinates.
Missing fields are unavailable in terminal output and null in JSON.
Exposure time uses seconds; focal length uses millimeters.
Capture time is the recorded EXIF DateTimeOriginal string, without timezone
conversion or a filesystem-date fallback.
.PP
JSON contains
.BR file ,
.BR image ,
.BR camera ,
.BR exposure ,
.BR date ,
and
.B location
objects.
The
.B has_gps
field is true only when both coordinates can be decoded as valid values.
False does not prove that all location tags are absent.
.SS duplicates
.B photoc duplicates
.I directory
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.PP
Report exact duplicate groups for regular files of any type.
Filter candidates by size and leading content, then compare SHA-256 digests.
No files are deleted or changed.
Visually similar images, or images with different metadata or encoding, are
not exact duplicates.
.PP
Report duplicate-group count, all files in those groups, and potential savings
assuming one retained copy per group.
Groups and paths have deterministic ordering.
Hard-linked paths can be counted separately; savings describe logical file
bytes, not guaranteed freed disk space.
File read failures are warned about and skipped; readable groups can still be
reported with exit status 1.
.PP
JSON contains
.BR directory ,
.BR recursive ,
.BR duplicate_group_count ,
.BR duplicate_file_count ,
.BR potential_savings_bytes ,
and
.BR groups .
Each group contains
.BR file_size_bytes ,
.BR sha256 ,
and
.BR paths .
.SS stats
.B photoc stats
.I directory
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.PP
Summarize parsed JPEGs, total/average file size, earliest/latest valid capture
dates, and camera-model, ISO, aperture, and focal-length distributions.
Missing fields are omitted from distributions.
Sort counts by descending frequency, then camera-model lexical order or
ascending numeric value for ties.
Most-used values are the first entries in those sorted distributions.
Percentages use all parsed photos as the denominator, so a distribution can
sum to less than 100 percent.
.PP
JPEGs without EXIF still contribute photo counts and file sizes.
Individual broken or unreadable JPEGs are warned about and counted as errors.
These per-file warnings alone do not change a successful exit status.
Inspect the scan summary for completeness.
.PP
JSON contains
.BR scan ,
.BR storage ,
.BR capture_dates ,
and
.B distributions
objects.
The photo count is
.BR scan.photos_parsed ;
the file-error count is
.BR scan.errors .
Empty distributions are arrays; unavailable dates and average size are null.
.SS rename
.B photoc rename
.I directory
.BI "\-\-format " template
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ \-\-apply ]
.PP
Preview JPEG renames using a filename template.
Files remain in their original directories.
Only
.B \-\-apply
authorizes changes after the entire plan passes preflight.
There is no separate dry-run flag.
.PP
Placeholders are
.BR {date} ,
.BR {datetime} ,
.BR {camera} ,
.BR {make} ,
.BR {iso} ,
.BR {aperture} ,
.BR {focal} ,
.BR {sequence} ,
.BR {original} ,
and
.BR {ext} .
Date and datetime use
.B YYYY-MM-DD
and
.BR YYYY-MM-DD_HH-MM-SS .
Camera is the model; original is the basename without its final extension.
Extension omits the dot and preserves source casing.
Sequence starts at 0001 in lexical source-path order across the scan.
Unsafe filename characters become underscores.
Use doubled braces for literal braces.
.PP
Missing required metadata, invalid JPEGs, existing destinations, and duplicate
proposed destinations block entries.
Any blocked entry prevents all changes with
.BR \-\-apply .
Dry runs still show safe rows and return status 1 when entries are blocked.
Report source-to-destination rows and planned/unchanged/blocked/applied/
rolled-back counts.
.SS sort
.B photoc sort
.I directory
.B \-\-by date|session
.RI [ "\-\-gap duration" ]
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ \-\-apply ]
.PP
Preview moves into directories under the input root while preserving filenames.
Date mode uses
.BR YYYY/MM/DD/ .
Session mode uses
.BR session-001/ ,
.BR session-002/ ,
and so on, ordered by capture time with source-path ties.
.PP
A session continues when the gap from the previous photo does not exceed the
threshold.
The default is 60 minutes.
.B \-\-gap
accepts nonnegative integer minutes or hours, such as
.B 30m
or
.BR 2h ,
and is valid only with session mode.
An exact-threshold gap remains in the same session.
IDs can change when the scanned set or gap changes.
.PP
Missing/invalid capture dates are blocked, without filesystem-date fallback.
Existing destinations, duplicate planned destinations, and unsafe parent
directories also block moves.
Any blocked entry prevents all moves with
.BR \-\-apply .
Dry run creates no directories.
Apply creates needed directories and uses exclusive moves; no copy-and-delete
fallback is provided across filesystems.
Report plan rows and summary counts as for
.BR rename .
.SS focus
.B photoc focus
.I file|directory
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ \-\-threshold " value" ]
.RI [ \-\-only\-blurry ]
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.PP
Report JPEG sharpness scores, sorted from lowest to highest with path ordering
for ties. Scores are variance of a Laplacian on grayscale pixels, using scaled
JPEG decoding with a maximum analysis dimension of 1024.
Scores strictly below
.B \-\-threshold
(default 100; finite and nonnegative) are labeled "possibly blurry".
.B \-\-only\-blurry
filters rows; summary counts and minimum/average/maximum cover all analyzed JPEGs.
The threshold is a review hint, not proof of blur or artistic quality.
Noise, texture, subject matter, and JPEG artifacts affect the score.
Missing EXIF is valid; directory scans skip non-JPEG files and continue past
unreadable or invalid JPEGs, returning status 1 if any JPEG fails.
JSON contains the input path, threshold, recursion and filtering settings,
a photos array with path, numeric score, threshold, and possibly_blurry boolean,
and summary counts and minimum/average/maximum scores.
Photo paths are relative to the scanned directory, or as supplied for a single
file. Summary scores are null when no JPEG could be analyzed.
Filtering does not change the summary; diagnostics remain on standard error.
.SS scrub
.B photoc scrub
.I file|directory
.B \-\-gps
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ \-\-in-place ]
.PP
Remove EXIF GPS directory entries and the GPS pointer.
By default, write a new copy with
.B .scrubbed
inserted before the original extension.
An existing copy destination causes failure without overwriting it.
Files without EXIF GPS are skipped without creating a copy.
Directory scans skip existing
.B .scrubbed
copies.
.B \-\-recursive
requires directory input.
.PP
JPEG image bytes are preserved without recompression.
Ordinary non-GPS EXIF is retained where possible; unusual MakerNotes may change.
Location data in XMP, MakerNotes, filenames, or visible content is outside the
removal scope.
Report processed, skipped, failed, and successfully GPS-removed file counts.
.PP
.B \-\-in-place
replaces each original only after writing, syncing, and verifying a temporary
JPEG beside it.
It creates no backup.
Symlinks, hard-linked files, differently owned files, and changed sources are
refused.
POSIX permission bits and group ownership are preserved; ACLs and extended
attributes are not copied.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Global options may appear before or after the command.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print global help, or command help when a command is supplied.
Exit successfully without executing the command.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
Cannot be combined with a command or
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Recognized as a request for detailed output; currently does not change output.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Recognized as a request for reduced output; currently does not change output.
Cannot be combined with verbose.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print structured JSON for
.BR exif ,
.BR stats ,
.BR duplicates ,
and
.BR focus .
Optional metadata is null, presence fields are booleans, and numbers remain
numeric.
No human-readable decorations are printed on standard output.
Warnings and errors remain on standard error; no JSON error envelope is emitted.
Other implemented commands reject this option with status 2.
.SH SAFETY
.B rename
and
.B sort
preview by default.
Apply checks the whole plan before changing files and never overwrites an
existing destination.
An apply failure triggers rollback attempts, but concurrent filesystem changes
or interruption can prevent full restoration.
They are not crash-safe transactions.
.PP
.B compress
and default
.B scrub
write new files immediately and preserve originals.
Temporary JPEGs are verified before finalizing without overwriting destinations.
Directory operations can retain earlier successful outputs after later failures.
.B scrub \-\-in-place
is explicit replacement with no backup or directory-wide rollback.
Keep backups and avoid concurrent changes when modifying important collections.
.SH EXAMPLES
Inspect a JPEG and save machine-readable metadata:
.PP
.nf
photoc exif photo.jpg
photoc exif photo.jpg \-\-json > metadata.json
.fi
.PP
Summarize a collection and report exact duplicates:
.PP
.nf
photoc stats ./photos \-\-recursive
photoc duplicates ./photos \-\-recursive \-\-json
.fi
.PP
Write compressed copies:
.PP
.nf
photoc compress photo.jpg \-\-quality 75
photoc compress photo.jpg \-\-target 2MB \-\-min-quality 30
photoc compress ./photos \-\-recursive \-\-output-dir ./compressed
.fi
.PP
Preview renames, then apply the reviewed naming scheme:
.PP
.nf
photoc rename ./photos \-\-format "{date}_{camera}_{sequence}.{ext}"
photoc rename ./photos \-\-format "{date}_{camera}_{sequence}.{ext}" \-\-apply
.fi
.PP
Preview date or session folders:
.PP
.nf
photoc sort ./photos \-\-by date \-\-recursive
photoc sort ./photos \-\-by session \-\-gap 30m
.fi
.PP
Remove EXIF GPS tags from a copy; explicit replacement requires a backup:
.PP
.nf
photoc scrub photo.jpg \-\-gps
photoc scrub photo.jpg \-\-gps \-\-in-place
.fi
.PP
Review possible low-sharpness photos:
.PP
.nf
photoc focus ./photos \-\-recursive \-\-threshold 100 \-\-only\-blurry
photoc focus ./photos \-\-recursive \-\-json > focus.json
.fi
.PP
Inspect a path beginning with a dash:
.PP
.nf
photoc exif \-\- \-photo.jpg
.fi
.SH FILES
.B photoc
does not currently read a configuration file or maintain a photo database.
.TP
.I *.compressed.jpg, *.compressed.jpeg
New compression outputs, with original extension casing retained.
.TP
.I *.scrubbed.jpg, *.scrubbed.jpeg
GPS-scrubbed copies beside originals, with extension casing retained.
.TP
.I YYYY/MM/DD/
Date-sort destination directories beneath the supplied scan root.
.TP
.I session-NNN/
Session-sort destination directories beneath the supplied scan root.
.TP
Temporary JPEGs
Created beside destinations for compression and metadata writing;
in-place scrub uses the source directory.
.TP
.I docs/
Command guides and JSON schemas in the source repository.
.TP
.I prefix/share/man/man1/photoc.1
Default installed manual location.
CMake's install prefix and install-directory settings can change this path.
.SH EXIT STATUSES
.TP
.B 0
Success, including empty collections, unchanged plans, missing optional EXIF,
and allowed skips.
.B stats
can return success with per-file warnings recorded in its scan summary.
.TP
.B 1
Operational failure, including unsupported input formats, invalid JPEGs,
I/O or output errors, blocked rename/sort plans, failed modifications, or
compression targets not met.
Partial reports or successful copies/modifications may already exist.
.B duplicates
returns this status after individual file-read warnings.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage: unknown command/option, missing arguments or values,
invalid option values, or incompatible options.
.TP
.B 3
Reserved for commands that are not implemented.
.SH BUGS
Image and metadata operations are JPEG-only.
Metadata summaries are not complete EXIF dumps, and metadata rewriting does
not guarantee preservation of every proprietary tag.
EXIF GPS removal is not removal of every possible location trace.
Capture timestamps are treated as recorded local times without timezone handling.
.PP
Report reproducible bugs at:
.UR https://github.com/ahmetomerv/photoc/issues
photoc issue tracker
.UE
.PP
Include the
.B photoc \-\-version
output, operating system, exact invocation, exit status, and relevant diagnostics.
Use a small synthetic or privacy-reviewed fixture when needed; avoid publishing
private photos or GPS information.
.SH PROJECT URL
.UR https://github.com/ahmetomerv/photoc
photoc project repository
.UE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR photoc\ \-\-help ,
the source repository's README and command guides under
.IR docs/ .
